-- EXPECT-ERROR: `Cap(IO.FileWrite)` used in module `ImplBody` but `IO.FileWrite` is not declared in `needs`
-- R8 AUDIT — an IMPL METHOD BODY is inside the `needs` walk (2026-08-06).
--
-- Companion to reject/t156, and a different walk: t156 is a method SIGNATURE
-- (the decl walk), this is an annotation inside a method BODY
-- (`cap_annots_in_expr`). A fix wired into only one of them still passes the
-- other's test.
--
-- Measured before this was closed, and the contrast is the sharpest statement
-- of the gap: the IDENTICAL `Cap(IO.FileWrite)` annotation produces an error
-- in a plain `fn` body and NOTHING AT ALL in an `impl` method body. Impl
-- bodies were darker than ordinary functions, which is backwards — an `impl`
-- is exactly where a dependency's capability use is least visible to a
-- reader.
--
-- A lambda parameter, not a `let`, for the same reason as reject/t151: it
-- names a capability without needing a value, so the file does not depend on
-- obtaining one and stays valid under R2.
mod ImplBody do
  needs IO.Console

  interface Runner(a) do
    fn run : a -> Int
  end

  type Box = Box(Int)

  impl Runner(Box) do
    fn run(b) do
      let take = fn (w : Cap(IO.FileWrite)) -> 1
      match b do
        Box(n) -> n
      end
    end
  end

  fn main() do
    println("the only capability here is inside an impl method body")
  end
end
